[break] a-hooks-own-stop-is-not-told
rule: A stop that a script hook's own program's look at the protected paths makes is told to the user at once and holds back the next prompt, as a hook command's is. Since a hook's program has no write root (the maintainers' call on the early review of the script hooks, M2), no look is made of it, and no hook's own stop can come: no test can reach this. It is kept, as the code that tells such a stop is, for a hook that is ever given a write root again.
